WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.450
USB-C was supposed to save us from the every-device-uses-a-different-cable
problem.

00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:06.540
What went wrong?

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:08.559
I love USB-C connectors.

00:00:08.559 --> 00:00:09.730
I love the symmetry.

00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:10.730
I love how they look.

00:00:10.730 --> 00:00:16.510
And yes, tons of phones and laptops have moved
to using USB-C...with one notable exception.

00:00:16.510 --> 00:00:17.870
This is really exciting.

00:00:17.870 --> 00:00:19.119
One cable that fits everything!

00:00:19.119 --> 00:00:23.930
Until you realize that all of these cables
look the same, but are not the same!

00:00:23.930 --> 00:00:26.980
Some will charge your device, but not transfer
any data.

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:30.120
Others will charge your smaller device, but
not your larger device.

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:32.750
Some work as monitor cables, others don't.

00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:35.220
Some can transfer up to 40 gigabits per second.

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:38.520
Others can only do 5 gigabits per second,
which is a huge difference if you're actually

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:40.740
trying to move large video files.

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:42.070
*deep breath* Okay.

00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:46.200
On top of that, some companies have added
their own proprietary tech into these USB-C

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:47.200
cables.

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:48.410
Apple looooves that proprietary tech.

00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:52.890
Even as a bona fide nerd gearhead, I still
find myself deeply frustrated by this.

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:54.830
I mean, just look at this ridiculous chart!

00:00:54.830 --> 00:00:55.830
Ugh!

00:00:55.830 --> 00:00:59.780
So, maybe going from this to this wasn't all
that it was cracked up to be.

